<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#esnaf">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Esnaf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Esnaf is a Turkish word meaning 'corporation', used for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> of craftsmen and traders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. In the early modern period guilds were an urban phenomenon: membership gave people a voice and was an important part of their identity, and their economic activity was controlled by political authorities. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> uprising of 1651 people made their voices heard as a group within their guilds, marched on the palace and, with a powerful religious leader on their side, forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> to step down. Unlike European guilds, which marginalised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Ottoman guilds mixed many religions, and many young women in the empire were hired to unwind cocoons, spin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and weave.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guilds</a> varied with society, social class and gender, and they had hierarchies of their own: craftsmen sometimes shared tools and worked together, and sometimes worked alone. In small towns craft structures could be simple, with one or a few kinds of tailors making fabric into ready-to-wear clothing, while in large cities producers were highly specialised and different guilds often made similar goods. A compound product such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> might pass through the hands of many craftsmen before it reached the consumer.</p><p>Collective workshops were favoured by the more substantial craftsmen and served as a means of social control. Every member was closely watched, and although some members were influential, none wholly controlled the work of the others. Partnerships existed, though how common they were is unknown. A more modern division of labour also appeared, in which bleachers, twisters and dyers owned both their own equipment and their own shops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries the Ottoman state created a system, based on an open-minded interpretation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>'s attitude toward rival monotheists, that allowed the inclusion of the Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of the conquered lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. Through conquest and immigration Ottoman society became highly mixed. In an economy so dominated by the state, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> were a major part of economic life, and the empire was divided into zones, so that people could buy only from the source to which they had been assigned.</p><p>Women worked both within and outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>. Besides those hired in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> work, there were groups of women cloth makers outside the guild system, and women exchanged goods among themselves in female markets. Estates of peasant women living near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in the late 16th century, which included some jewellery, point to links with the market.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>It has often been assumed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> declined because they held back innovation, but their decline came mainly from political causes, since political authorities took part in production and controlled its economic conditions. Guilds are also often associated only with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and with medieval times, yet they existed before the medieval era and formed a large part of the economy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Esnaf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Craftsmen and shopkeepers belonged to guilds (<b>esnaf</b>), which kept up standards, limited production and helped members in hard times, and through which the state watched over the bazaars and the townspeople; by the 1580s the guilds were a fixture of town life, as the account of the great…</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li></ul></section>
